      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0000-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:02 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: THOMAS C. GOLDSTEIN, ESQ., Washington, D.C.; for Petitioner. CARTER G. PHILLIPS, ESQ.; Washington, D.C.; for Respondents. NICOLE A. SAHARSKY, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Thomas C. Goldstein",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOLDSTEIN: Mr. Chief Justice, may it please the Court: We ask this Court to hold that a jail may strip search an arrestee in cases of reasonable suspicion. That is the rule that was applied throughout almost the entire country in the three decades after Bell v. Wolfish, without either administrative difficulty or any apparent increase in smuggling. We're here today, of course, because both the Burlington Jail and the Essex County Jail require every arrestee to stand 2 feet in front of a correctional officer and strip naked."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Do you apply the reasonable suspicion rule to all arrestees? I thought you were making a distinction between felons and less serious offenders."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, my understanding of the statistics -- and correct me if I'm wrong -- is that they get about 70 new people going through this process a day. Is there anything in the record about how much additional time it would require to look at each one, to look at their record, to determine which category they should fall into, to strip search or not, as opposed to having a blanket rule?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Goldstein",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOLDSTEIN: Sure. There is because they do this already. They -- it is not an administrative problem. They apply our rule today. Remember, Mr. Chief Justice, when he arrived at the Burlington County Jail, they did an assessment of him and determined that there was no reasonable suspicion. The jails in this case did pull up his prior criminal history, and they have no problem doing that. They apply our standard today. It is not a difficult one."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: That you don't have to have visitors. Can you really condition your -- your having visitors on your waiver of your Fourth Amendment rights?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Phillips, if I could understand your position, you think that there's no reasonable suspicion even for that more intrusive body cavity search; is that right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: So, why do we change the policy? In Bell, we found that the policy was successful. Even though there were searches, contraband still got in. So, virtually every circuit -- in practice, the Federal system -- have been following this reasonable suspicion for minor crimes, and they've been fairly successful. So, why do we change the constitutional rule to let them do more?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0002-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Kentucky, there is today -- the single biggest problem in Kentucky prisons and the biggest cause of death is drug overdose, which suggests that there's a serious contraband issue in Kentucky."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: But we don't have 20/20. We have how many years? Fifteen years since Bell -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. SAHARSKY",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SAHARSKY: That may be true, but it's not the information that the people who do intake and are doing the searches have. They do not have that information at their fingertips in the Federal system."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-10-945",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "f3a188a0fd577d14288685e74d750a2f1a66d0820fe5a1e42f59a8f4f50cd331",
      "evidence_id": "ev-f3a188a0fd57-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
